\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned to allocate 50\% of attention to system-prompt goals (GAR\,=\,0.50 under teacher forcing), Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct retains only GAR\,=\,0.064 under autoregressive generation---an 8$\times$ collapse. We trace this failure to exposure bias: attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text. ThreadGate, per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates trained with an auxiliary GAR loss, provides a controlled testbed. A five-way comparison isolates turn conditioning, gating, gradient conflict, and fine-tuning; each pair kills one hypothesis. Gating outperforms LoRA-only ($p$\,=\,0.004) but the gap remains 7.8$\times$; PCGrad eliminates gradient conflict but \emph{worsens} AR GAR ($p$\,=\,0.002), ruling out the most intuitive failure mechanism by \emph{modus tollens}. Steering ($\beta$\,=\,5) recovers GAR to 0.727; scheduled sampling compresses the gap to 1.44$\times$ (single seed). Cross-model evaluation on Llama-3.1-8B confirms a 27$\times$ gap. These findings establish that exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention losses while NTP self-recovers---a failure mode without prior multi-turn transformer characterization.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Fine-tuned to allocate 50\% of its attention to system-prompt goals (GAR\,=\,0.50 under teacher forcing), Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct retains only GAR\,=\,0.064 under autoregressive generation---an 8$\times$ collapse that widens to 27$\times$ on Llama-3.1-8B. This paper traces the failure to exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}: attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text, even when next-token prediction (NTP) remains robust.

Multi-turn attention decay is well-documented---\citet{dongre2026attention} showed that the Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR), measuring attention to system-prompt goal tokens, declines 27--48\% across 12 models (we observe 70.8\% on Qwen-2.5-7B) while residual stream probes retain goal content (AUC\,=\,0.98)---but three research gaps remain open. To our knowledge, no work examines how per-layer transformer behavior varies across conversation turns (the \emph{turn$\times$depth} gap). Existing remediations---split-softmax \citep{li2024measuring}, StreamingLLM \citep{xiao2024streaming}, gated attention \citep{qiu2025gated}---operate at inference time or are turn-agnostic; \emph{training-time} multi-turn attention optimization has not been attempted. And classical exposure bias mitigation \citep{bengio2015scheduled,lamb2016professor} targets RNN sequence generation; no \emph{multi-turn transformer equivalent} exists.

ThreadGate addresses these gaps with per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates on the residual stream: $x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$, where $g_l$ is a two-layer network conditioned on hidden states and turn position. A five-way controlled comparison---ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base---isolates turn conditioning, gating, gradient conflict, and fine-tuning (\S\ref{sec:comparison-design}), where each adjacent pair kills exactly one alternative hypothesis.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_headline.pdf}
  \caption{Exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention losses. (a)~Under TF evaluation, all fine-tuned variants converge to GAR-ours$\approx$0.50; under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR collapses 7.8--16.7$\times$. Log scale; dashed slopes connect paired TF-AR values. (b)~Inference-time $\beta$-steering recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation---not representation degradation---as the proximal cause (9\% PPL cost at $\beta$\,=\,3).}
  \label{fig:headline}
\end{figure*}

Training succeeds: all variants converge to TF GAR\,=\,0.50 (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}a). AR GAR collapses to 0.019--0.064 (8--17$\times$). Gating outperforms LoRA-only ($p$\,=\,0.004) yet the gap remains 7.8$\times$. PCGrad eliminates gradient conflict but \emph{worsens} AR GAR ($p$\,=\,0.002), ruling it out by \emph{modus tollens}. NTP self-recovers \citep{he2019exposure} because prediction is the same task at both regimes; GAR optimizes a prefix property with no AR counterpart. Steering ($\beta$\,=\,5) recovers GAR to 0.727; scheduled sampling compresses the gap to 1.44$\times$ (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Our contributions: (1)~first empirical measurement of exposure bias on auxiliary attention losses (8--27$\times$ across two architectures); (2)~PCGrad-based causal exclusion of gradient conflict ($p$\,=\,0.002, effect reverses); (3)~first evidence that scheduled sampling transfers to multi-turn transformer attention objectives (gap 7.8$\times$$\to$1.44$\times$); (4)~cross-architecture validation on Llama-3.1-8B confirming generalization.

\section{Training Configuration}\label{sec:appendix-hyper}

Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams} lists the full training configuration shared across all fine-tuned variants. ThreadGate gate parameters receive a $10\times$ learning rate multiplier and are initialized with bias 1.5 ($\sigma(1.5) \approx 0.82$); the gate MLP adds ${\sim}$57K parameters per variant ($<$0.001\% of 7B). The scheduled sampling variant (v8b) linearly increases the model-generated context ratio from 0 to 0.5 over 3 epochs (29.7--37.1\% effective usage due to per-sample Bernoulli sampling). All experiments were conducted on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB VRAM), requiring 6--8 hours per variant.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{Training hyperparameters for all fine-tuned variants. Entries marked $\dagger$ apply only to ThreadGate and its ablations.}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\begin{tabular}{l@{\hspace{8pt}}l}
\toprule
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Base model & Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct \\
LoRA rank / $\alpha$ & 16 / 32 \\
LoRA targets & \texttt{q\_proj}, \texttt{v\_proj} \\
Optimizer & AdamW ($\beta_1$\!=\!0.9, $\beta_2$\!=\!0.999) \\
Learning rate & $2 \times 10^{-4}$ \\
Gate LR mult.$^\dagger$ & $10\times$ \\
Batch / grad accum & 1 / 8 (eff.\ 8) \\
Epochs & 3 \\
Max seq length & 2048 \\
Training data & 8K UltraChat convs \\
Seed & 42 (+123 for TG, SS) \\
$\lambda_\text{GAR}$/$\lambda_\text{div}$ & 1.0 / 1.0 \\
GAR target $\tau$ & 0.5 \\
Gate init bias$^\dagger$ & 1.5 ($\sigma\!\approx\!0.82$) \\
Gate hidden dim$^\dagger$ & 64 \\
Turn embed dim$^\dagger$ & 16 \\
GPU & RTX PRO 6000 (102\,GB) \\
Training time & 6--8 h / variant \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Loss Formulation Search}\label{sec:appendix-loss}

The final MSE + gate diversity loss (\S\ref{sec:loss}) emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families. Table~\ref{tab:loss-iterations} summarizes the progression. The search was motivated by a consistent failure mode: naive formulations either collapse to trivial solutions (cumulative losses) or drive gates toward identity (maximize-family losses). We describe each family below.

\paragraph{Cumulative losses (v1--v2).} These losses accumulate GAR contributions across layers, rewarding monotonically increasing attention. v1 collapsed because once the cumulative GAR exceeded the threshold, the loss gradient vanished and gates froze. v2 added an $\varepsilon$-stabilization term to the cumulative product, but numerical instability from the product of 28 per-layer terms dominated, and training followed the same trajectory.

\paragraph{Maximize losses (v3--v5).} These losses directly maximize mean GAR ($-\text{mean}(\text{GAR})$). The unbounded objective rewards full pass-through ($g \to 1.0$), collapsing gate diversity (std $<$ 0.025). At $\lambda$=2 (v3), gates froze within 100 steps; at $\lambda$=10 (v3b), convergence to identity accelerated; at $\lambda$=1 (v5), rebalancing delayed but did not prevent the same degenerate solution.

\paragraph{Smooth saturation (v6).} This bounded the reward using a saturating function, intended to prevent the unbounded growth of maximize losses. In practice, the loss landscape remained monotonic in $g$, and training followed the same terminal trajectory as the maximize family, only more slowly.

\paragraph{MSE + gate diversity (v7).} The MSE formulation provides bidirectional pressure: GAR above $\tau$ incurs the same penalty as GAR below $\tau$, preventing the runaway optimization of maximize losses. The gate diversity regularizer ($\mathcal{L}_\text{div}$) enforces a minimum standard deviation across per-layer gate values, preventing all gates from collapsing to a uniform value. This combination is the only one that achieved stable convergence (GAR-ours = 0.50, PPL = 2.78).

